### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1. **The fairness claim is not supported because the method assumes protected-attribute labels at distillation time and is evaluated only in that fully supervised setting.**  
   **Classification:** Structural  
   **Concerned parts:** Abstract (“ensuring that models trained on condensed datasets are unbiased towards protected attributes”), Sec. 4.2 Eq. (7), Conclusion/Limitation (lines 534–536).  
   The core method requires partitioning the training set by protected attribute and synchronizing matching across those groups. That is not a minor implementation detail; it is the mechanism. So the paper is not solving “fair dataset distillation” in general, but “dataset distillation with access to protected-attribute annotations and group-balanced objectives.” The abstract and conclusion repeatedly state a much broader claim. This matters because in many fairness settings PA labels are incomplete, unavailable, or legally constrained; the method simply does not apply there. The limitation paragraph admits this dependence, but only at the end, after broad claims of a general FDD framework. This is a scope mismatch in the central claim, not a missing experiment.

2. **The theoretical guarantees do not establish the paper’s main empirical claims about fairness of trained classifiers.**  
   **Classification:** Structural  
   **Concerned parts:** Theorem 4.1, Theorem 4.2, Sec. 4.2 overall; fairness metric defined in Sec. 2.  
   The theorems are about the location of the *mean target signal* of synthetic samples and an upper-bound relation between matching objectives. But the fairness metric used in experiments is DEO of the *trained classifier predictions*. There is no theorem connecting equal weighting of PA-group feature/gradient means in Eq. (7) to equalized-odds fairness of the downstream classifier. In fact, matching a single synthetic class set to the arithmetic mean of PA-group centers can easily preserve or even encode PA structure if groups differ multimodally or nonlinearly; “centered between groups” is not equivalent to “fair” under EO. Likewise, Theorem 4.2’s upper-bound argument only says FairDD minimizes a stronger matching objective than vanilla DD under convex distances; it does not imply “comprehensive distribution coverage” in any rigorous sense relevant to accuracy or fairness. The paper’s theoretical section therefore overclaims: it does not prove fairness guarantees for the quantity actually measured.

3. **The experimental design does not isolate whether gains come from fairness-aware distillation rather than simply using PA labels to rebalance supervision.**  
   **Classification:** Evidential  
   **Concerned parts:** Sec. 5.1 Baselines, Tables 1–5, claims of “consistent superiority” and “versatile FDD approach” in Abstract/Conclusion.  
   FairDD is compared only to vanilla DD methods and random sampling. But FairDD’s objective injects group supervision directly; the natural question is whether similar or better fairness would be obtained by much simpler PA-aware baselines, e.g. stratified/random balanced subset selection by (TA, PA), balanced per-group condensation budget, group-reweighted DD loss, or training-time fairness reweighting on the distilled set. Without such controls, the experiments do not establish that the proposed synchronized matching is the important ingredient. This is especially important because the reported gains are enormous on synthetic datasets (e.g. DM accuracy jumping from ~25 to ~95 on C-MNIST in Table 2), suggesting that vanilla DD may simply be catastrophically mismatched to the biased setup while any PA-balanced procedure would help dramatically. As written, the paper supports “using PA information helps” much more strongly than “this specific framework is a generally effective fair distillation method.”

4. **Several headline conclusions are driven mainly by heavily constructed synthetic bias settings, with limited evidence on realistic fairness scenarios.**  
   **Classification:** Evidential  
   **Concerned parts:** Sec. 5.1 datasets, Tables 1–3, broad claims in Abstract and Sec. 7.  
   Most evidence comes from colored MNIST/Fashion-MNIST variants and a grayscale CIFAR construction where PA is artificially injected and the test distribution is explicitly balanced by design. Those are useful stress tests, but they are not enough to support broad claims about fairness in image recognition. The only real-world dataset shown in the main paper is CelebA with one TA/PA pair, and performance there is much less clean. A paper making a general claim about fair dataset distillation needs stronger evidence in realistic settings; otherwise the claim should be narrowed to synthetic spurious-correlation benchmarks plus one facial-attribute case.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Introduction
- The abstract claims FairDD “can be seamlessly applied to diverse matching-based DD approaches” and “improves fairness … without sacrificing classification accuracy.” The body only evaluates DM/DC/IDC/DREAM-style methods and explicitly excludes TM. “Diverse” is true within one family, but the framing sounds broader than the evidence.
- The introduction says FairDD works “regardless of PA imbalance in the original data.” That should be qualified: only when PA labels are known and can be used to group the data.
- The claim that this is the “first” work on fairness in DD may be true, but it is not substantiated in a way that matters for acceptance; I would not weight this heavily.

### Section 2: Preliminaries
- The fairness discussion is underdeveloped relative to the claims. The paper adopts equalized-odds-style disparity metrics, but later the method optimizes only group-wise signal matching. The missing conceptual bridge between optimization target and fairness criterion is important.
- The notation suggests idealized equivalence of risks in Eq. (2), but this plays no role later beyond motivation.

### Section 3: A close look at dataset distillation from fairness
- Eq. (6) is too strong as an explanation of why vanilla DD is unfair. Even if the optimum of the matching objective equals a weighted average of group expectations, that does not by itself imply the realized synthetic dataset will “produce more samples belonging to” the majority PA group. The argument conflates mean feature matching with sample-level group composition.
- The statement “minority groups experience higher loss during testing” is asserted rather than derived from Eqs. (4)–(6). This is a conceptual leap.

### Section 4: FairDD
- Eq. (7) uses one synthetic class set \(\mathcal S_y\) matched simultaneously to all PA-group means. That is a reasonable heuristic, but it is not obvious that a single set can represent all groups well when groups are far apart or multimodal. This limitation should be discussed because it directly bears on the mechanism.
- Theorem 4.1 is not really a fairness theorem; it is an optimization stationary-point observation about mean signals under certain distances.
- Theorem 4.2 overinterprets an upper bound as “guarantee the comprehensive distribution coverage.” Minimizing an upper bound does not generally imply better coverage, only a sufficient condition on the surrogate objective.
- The “pull-and-push” language is intuitive, but there is no actual repulsion term in Eq. (7); all terms are attraction to group means. The description is stronger than the objective.

### Section 5.1: Experiment setup
- The paper says all baselines are reproduced from official implementations, but key fairness-relevant training details are not visible in the main paper: number of seeds, variance reporting, model selection criterion, and whether hyperparameters were retuned for biased datasets. Given the very large swings in Tables 1–2, variance matters.
- Reporting only point estimates is a weakness throughout. With distilled datasets and random network initializations, instability is expected.

### Section 5.2: Main results
- Tables 1 and 2 show extremely large gains, especially on colored MNIST/FMNIST. This makes the missing PA-aware control baselines more serious, not less.
- On CelebA, “without sacrificing accuracy” is not uniformly true in the stronger sense of matching the full-data baseline; distilled-set accuracy remains well below Whole in Table 2. The paper’s wording should be more careful.
- Table 3 is hard to interpret and seems inconsistently formatted, but ignoring parser artifacts, the intended message is cross-architecture transfer. Still, it only compares DM vs DM+FairDD. This is too narrow to support “generalization to diverse architectures” as a broad property of the framework.

### Section 5.3: Result analysis
- The t-SNE and feature visualizations are illustrative only. They are presented somewhat causally (“eliminates the recognition of PA”, “produces compact TA representations”), but such conclusions are not warranted from t-SNE plots alone.
- The feature-coverage visualization uses the same random network employed in distillation. That is aligned with the method but weak as evidence for downstream generalization.

### Section 5.4: Ablation study
- The ablation section text mentions BR values \(\{0.85,0.90,0.95\}\), but Table 4 in the main text appears to show only 0.85 and 0.90. If 0.95 is only in appendix, the main-text claim should not overstate what is shown here.
- Initialization ablations are useful, but again only for DM vs DM+FairDD. They do not isolate the necessity of synchronized matching versus simpler balanced objectives.

### Related Work / Conclusion
- The conclusion repeats claims of “guarantees the fairness of synthetic datasets while maintaining representativeness.” That overstates both the theoretical results and the empirical scope.
- The limitation about needing PA labels is important and should have been foregrounded much earlier, because it materially changes the problem setting being solved.

## Strengths

- The paper identifies a real and previously underexplored problem: dataset distillation can worsen bias when the original data are PA-imbalanced.
- The proposed modification is simple and easy to graft onto matching-based DD objectives; that practical simplicity is a genuine strength.
- The empirical effect sizes against vanilla DD are large and consistent across several matching-based backbones and IPC budgets.
- The paper does more than just report fairness metrics; it also checks target-task accuracy and some cross-architecture transfer, which is the right evaluation instinct for distilled datasets.

## Overall Assessment

The paper tackles an important problem and proposes a simple idea with striking empirical improvements over vanilla matching-based dataset distillation. But the submission overclaims in two central ways: it presents a PA-label-dependent method as a broadly applicable fair dataset distillation framework, and it presents theory about mean-signal matching as if it guaranteed downstream equalized-odds fairness and distribution coverage. On the experimental side, the absence of simple PA-aware control baselines means the paper does not show that synchronized matching, rather than merely injecting protected-attribute supervision, is the real source of the gains. My judgment is that the core empirical observation is interesting, but the paper in its current form does not establish its strongest claims and should not be accepted without a substantial reframing and stronger evidence.
### Neutral Reviewer
## Balanced Review

### Summary
This paper proposes FairDD, a framework that integrates fairness into dataset distillation by introducing "synchronized matching" — aligning the synthetic dataset equally with each protected attribute (PA) group rather than with the overall distribution dominated by majority groups. The key modification reformulates the vanilla matching objective from a weighted sum over PA groups (Eq. 4) to an unweighted sum (Eq. 7), ensuring each PA group contributes equally to the generation of synthetic samples. Theoretical analysis and extensive experiments across multiple datasets and distillation methods demonstrate significant fairness improvements without sacrificing accuracy.

### Strengths
1. **Novel and important problem formulation.** The paper identifies a previously unexplored issue: vanilla DD methods inherit and amplify biases from original datasets due to PA imbalance (Eq. 6 shows synthetic signals converge to ratio-weighted averages). This is a meaningful contribution to the DD literature, as evidenced by the dramatic fairness gaps (e.g., DEO_M of 100.0 for DM vs. 10.10 for the whole dataset on C-MNIST FG at IPC=10).

2. **Simple, general, and architecture-agnostic method.** FairDD requires no modifications to existing DD architectures — it only reformulates the optimization objective at the group level. The framework is successfully applied to both Distribution Matching (DM) and Gradient Matching (DC, IDC, DREAM), demonstrating broad applicability.

3. **Strong theoretical grounding.** Theorem 4.1 rigorously shows that synchronized matching removes dependence on sample ratios, causing synthetic signals to converge to the unweighted center of all PA groups. Theorem 4.2 establishes that FairDD's objective upper-bounds the vanilla objective (via Jensen's inequality), guaranteeing distributional coverage is at least as comprehensive.

4. **Comprehensive experimental evaluation.** Experiments span five datasets with different bias types (FG, BG, BG&FG, real-world), four DD methods, three IPC settings, cross-architecture generalization, and ablations on bias ratio and initialization. FairDD consistently reduces DEO_M/A by large margins (e.g., 100.0→17.04 on C-MNIST FG) while maintaining or improving accuracy.

5. **Insightful visualizations.** Figure 4 clearly shows improved feature coverage, and Figure 5's t-SNE analysis effectively demonstrates that FairDD eliminates PA sensitivity while producing more compact TA representations.

### Weaknesses
1. **Core method is a simple reweighting that has strong precedents in fair learning.** The transition from Eq. 4 to Eq. 7 is essentially removing the sample ratio weights — equivalent to group-balanced loss, a well-known technique in fair ML and long-tail learning. The paper does not adequately discuss this connection or explain why this particular instantiation in the DD context constitutes a substantial methodological contribution beyond applying known reweighting ideas.

2. **Mostly synthetic/constructed benchmarks with limited real-world evaluation.** Four of five datasets are artificially constructed (C-MNIST, C-FMNIST, CIFAR10-S), and even CelebA uses a simple binary PA (gender). The paper lacks evaluation on datasets with more complex, multi-valued, or intersectional protected attributes where the "equal weighting" assumption may be less straightforward.

3. **No comparison with fairness-aware baselines.** The paper does not compare against applying standard fairness pre-processing methods (e.g., resampling, reweighting the original data before distillation, or adding fairness regularization terms to the DD objective). It is unclear whether the gains come specifically from the synchronized matching formulation or simply from any mechanism that balances PA groups.

4. **Theoretical assumptions are strong and not validated.** The proofs assume convergence to optimal solutions where the gradient equals zero (Eq. 5, 8), which rarely holds in practice with finite steps, SGD optimization, and non-convex landscapes. The practical implications of these assumptions not holding are not discussed.

5. **Limited applicability and acknowledged dependency on PA labels.** The method requires PA annotations during distillation, and trajectory matching is explicitly excluded. The paper acknowledges this limitation but does not explore alternatives (e.g., pseudo-labeling) empirically, even though they are mentioned as future work.

6. **Accuracy degradation in some settings is not well analyzed.** In C-FMNIST (BG) at IPC=100, DC+FairDD drops accuracy from 62.70 to 71.76 (Table 2), and cross-architecture results show accuracy declining with model capacity (e.g., CIFAR10-S: 58.84→50.93 from ConvNet to ResNet18). The explanation offered (lines 419–424) is speculative and not empirically validated.

### Novelty & Significance
The novelty lies primarily in the problem formulation — identifying and formally analyzing how DD amplifies PA bias — rather than in the method itself, which is a straightforward application of group balancing. The theoretical analysis (especially Eq. 6's derivation of why vanilla DD converges to biased solutions) is a valuable contribution. The significance is moderate-to-high: as dataset distillation gains practical importance, ensuring fairness of distilled datasets is crucial, and this paper provides an effective and simple first solution. However, the methodological contribution is somewhat incremental given existing group-balancing techniques in fair ML.

### Suggestions for Improvement
1. **Add comparisons with fairness baselines.** Include at least: (a) oversampling/resampling the original dataset to balance PA groups before distillation, (b) applying a fairness regularization term (e.g., equalized odds constraint) during distillation, and (c) simple reweighting of the loss by inverse group frequency. This would clarify whether the gains are specific to the synchronized matching formulation.

2. **Strengthen real-world evaluation.** Add experiments on datasets with more complex PA structures (e.g., UTK Face with multiple race categories, or datasets with intersectional attributes). Evaluate on more than a single binary PA on CelebA.

3. **Relax theoretical assumptions and provide empirical convergence analysis.** Track how closely the optimal solution conditions are met during actual training. Show convergence curves for both FairDD and vanilla DD objectives to validate the theoretical claims empirically.

4. **Discuss connections to group-balanced learning more explicitly.** Acknowledge the relationship to reweighting/group-balancing methods in long-tail and fair learning literature, and articulate what is uniquely gained by applying this within the DD matching framework (e.g., the "pull-and-push" effect on synthetic sample generation).

5. **Investigate the impact of the number of PA groups.** As the number of PA groups increases, the equal-weighting assumption may become problematic (e.g., if some groups have very few samples, matching to their noisy centroids could hurt both fairness and accuracy). An ablation on the number of PA groups would be informative.

6. **Address the trajectory matching exclusion more thoroughly.** Either provide empirical evidence for the claimed overfitting issue with minority group trajectories, or explore hybrid approaches (e.g., using FairDD for initial synthetic data generation followed by trajectory refinement).
### Spark
## How to Improve This Paper

### Missing Experiments (top 3-5 only)
1. **Comparison with simple reweighting baseline.** The most natural alternative to FairDD's synchronized matching is reweighting the loss terms in Eq. 4 by $1/r_y^{a_i}$ (inverse PA group size). Without this comparison, it is unclear whether the proposed group-level reformulation offers any advantage over trivial reweighting, which directly addresses the same imbalance problem.

2. **More real-world datasets with natural bias.** Only CelebA has real-world bias; the other four datasets have synthetically injected bias (color, grayscale) that is easily separable and may make the problem unrealistically easy. Add datasets like UTKFace, FairFace, or Waterbirds to test whether FairDD works when bias is entangled with semantics rather than superficially injected.

3. **Comparison with fairness methods applied at training time.** A critical missing baseline: train on vanilla distilled data but apply standard fairness interventions (e.g., adversarial debiasing, fair regularization, or simple oversampling at training). If applying fairness during training on vanilla distilled data achieves similar or better results, the value of modifying the distillation itself is undermined.

4. **Ablation on number of protected attribute groups.** The synchronized matching pulls synthetic samples toward all PA group centers simultaneously. With many PA groups (e.g., >5), the opposing gradient directions could cause convergence issues. This scalability question is never tested.

### Deeper Analysis Needed (top 3-5 only)
1. **The dramatic accuracy improvements demand explanation.** FairDD improves DM accuracy on C-MNIST (FG) IPC=10 from 25.01% to 94.61%. This is not a marginal gain—it suggests the vanilla DD baseline is effectively broken on these biased benchmarks. If FairDD's main effect is fixing a collapsed optimization rather than selectively improving fairness, the framing of the contribution changes substantially. Analyze whether the vanilla baselines converge properly.

2. **Theorem 4.2's guarantee is weaker than claimed.** The paper states that $\mathcal{L}_{\text{FairDD}}$ is an upper bound of $\mathcal{L}$, and thus "minimizing $\mathcal{L}_{\text{FairDD}}$ ensures the minimization of $\mathcal{L}$." This logic is flawed: minimizing an upper bound does not guarantee minimizing the original function unless the bound is tight. The bound here is loose (it drops all $r_y^{a_i}$ weights), so this guarantee does not hold. Either tighten the analysis or moderate the claim.

3. **Sensitivity to PA label noise.** FairDD assumes perfect PA labels during distillation. In practice, protected attribute annotations can be noisy or incomplete. Without analyzing how label errors propagate through the synchronized matching, it is unclear how deployable this method is.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Failure cases of FairDD.** No analysis of when FairDD underperforms or fails. Show cases where synchronized matching produces poor synthetic samples (e.g., when PA groups have highly overlapping features, or when the test-time fairness metric does not improve despite training-time adjustments).

2. **t-SNE of synthetic samples for datasets with >2 PA groups.** Current visualizations only cover binary or few-group settings. Visualizing the feature space when many PA groups compete to pull synthetic samples would reveal whether the method produces degenerate or well-distributed representations.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Run reweighting baselines.** Replace the synchronized matching with $r_y^{a_i}$-reweighted matching in Eq. 4 and compare. This is the minimal experiment needed to justify the specific design of FairDD over simpler alternatives.

2. **Validate vanilla DD baselines on unbiased versions of these datasets.** If DM achieves only 25% on C-MNIST (FG) because the bias breaks the method rather than FairDD being special, this needs to be shown. Run vanilla DD on the same datasets with BR=0.1 (balanced) to confirm baselines work correctly.

3. **Test on Trajectory Matching despite the stated difficulty.** The paper dismisses TM due to overfitting on minority groups, but does not verify this experimentally. A small experiment showing the failure mode would strengthen the argument for excluding it.

### Other stuff you can add
1. **Report standard deviations across multiple runs.** All results appear to be single-run numbers. Given that dataset distillation is known to have high variance, reporting std over 3-5 runs is essential for establishing statistical significance of the fairness improvements.
2. **Clarify the fairness metric direction for Table 1.** DEO_M and DEO_A are defined as differences (lower is better), but the "Whole" baseline sometimes shows higher DEO than distilled methods on CelebA, which is counterintuitive. Verify and discuss these cases.
### Human Finder
Based on my thorough analysis of the paper and retrieval of relevant human reviews, here are the strengths and weaknesses:

---

**Strengths:**

1. The paper identifies an important and previously overlooked problem in dataset distillation: the amplification of protected attribute bias during the distillation process. The empirical demonstration that vanilla DDs exacerbate PA imbalance (e.g., DEO_M reaching 100.0 for C-MNIST vs. ~10 for the whole dataset) is a compelling motivation.

2. The proposed FairDD framework is elegantly simple — it reformulates the class-level matching objective into PA-wise group-level synchronized matching (Eq. 7) without requiring modifications to existing DD architectures or introducing additional hyperparameters/learnable parameters. This simplicity and modularity make it broadly applicable.

3. The theoretical analysis (Theorems 4.1 and 4.2) provides formal guarantees that synchronized matching mitigates PA imbalance (by showing the expectation converges to the arithmetic mean of all PA groups, independent of sample ratios) while maintaining distributional coverage (by proving FairDD's loss upper-bounds vanilla DD's loss via Jensen's inequality).

4. Consistent improvements across diverse DD paradigms (DM, DC, IDC, DREAM) and datasets (synthetic and real-world CelebA), with both fairness gains and maintained/improved accuracy, demonstrate the versatility and effectiveness of the approach.

---

**Weaknesses:**

1. **Over-reliance on synthetically biased datasets limits practical validation.** Among the five datasets, four (C-MNIST FG/BG, C-FMNIST FG/BG, CIFAR10-S) are artificially constructed with injected color/grayscale biases, and only CelebA represents a real-world scenario. The performance of FairDD on more complex real-world datasets with naturally occurring, multi-faceted biases remains unverified. As noted by reviewers of closely related work on bias in dataset distillation: *"The method proposed in this paper seems to excel only on very simple datasets with sufficiently significant biases (e.g., CMNIST and BG FMNIST). On more complex datasets that lack remarkable biases… the performance appears to be modest. This raises concerns about the practicality and robustness"* (from review of L5yq5KsnEZ, Reviewer 2). Additionally: *"The experimental benchmarks are limited to the synthetic datasets. In recent research, several real-world datasets have been employed for evaluation, such as CelebA-HQ, IMDB, and BFFHQ"* (L5yq5KsnEZ, Reviewer 1).

2. **FairDD requires access to protected attribute labels during distillation, limiting its applicability.** As the paper acknowledges in the Limitation section, FairDD relies on PA prior information to conduct attribute-wise matching. In many real-world settings, protected attribute annotations may be unavailable, unreliable, or legally restricted. Reviewers of related fairness-in-distillation work raised similar concerns: *"The model currently focuses on addressing bias with respect to single sensitive attribute, potentially overlooking complex bias scenarios involving multiple intersecting attributes"* (iRgzG5DKgA, Reviewer 3). Related work on fairness with group information also noted: *"In most applications we have multi-valued multiple protected attributes. It seems that it is non-trivial to extend the loss function to cater to such datasets"* (TmKeT3IFTZ, Reviewer 4).

3. **Exclusion of Trajectory Matching (TM) limits the framework's claimed generality.** FairDD is presented as a "versatile" and "generalist" FDD approach, but it explicitly excludes TM methods (e.g., MTT, TESLA), which are among the state-of-the-art DD methods. The justification that TM requires extra model trajectories trained on minority groups that would suffer from overfitting is plausible but not empirically validated. Reviewers of related DD work raised analogous concerns: *"I am wondering why this work did not attempt to combine the proposed reweighting strategy with the state-of-the-art matching training trajectory (MTT) method"* (L5yq5KsnEZ, Reviewer 2), and further: *"What are the reasons why the proposed debiasing methods do not work for dataset distillation methods that only rely on expert model training trajectories?"* (L5yq5KsnEZ, Reviewer 3).

4. **Unfair comparison with baselines that lack access to protected attribute information.** FairDD leverages PA labels as additional supervision during distillation, while the vanilla DD baselines do not use this information. This creates an asymmetry that makes it difficult to attribute improvements solely to the synchronized matching design rather than the extra PA information. A reviewer of related fairness work noted: *"while the proposed technique (FADIG) is shown to perform well, it has an unfair advantage because it has the extra information about the under-represented groups that is not available to other algorithms"* (TmKeT3IFTZ, Reviewer 3). Including baselines that are also given PA information (e.g., simple reweighting with PA labels) would provide a more informative comparison.

5. **Computational overhead of FairDD is not discussed.** The synchronized matching in Eq. 7 replaces a single distance computation per class with |A| distance computations per class (one per PA group), increasing the per-iteration cost proportionally to the number of PA groups. Related work on subgroup-aware DD raised similar concerns: *"The proposed DRO approach, particularly with clustering and CVaR, may introduce significant computational overhead. This added complexity could be amplified for large-scale datasets … However, the paper does not discuss the computational overhead of the proposed method"* (3JsU5QXNru, Reviewer 1). The paper mentions "Additional computation overhead is provided in Appendix C" but this is not discussed in the main text, and the scalability to scenarios with many PA groups is unclear.

6. **Accuracy gap with the full (uncondensed) dataset remains substantial for complex datasets.** While FairDD improves accuracy over vanilla DDs, there is still a notable gap compared to the "Whole" dataset baseline on harder benchmarks — e.g., CIFAR10-S: 58.84 vs. 69.78 at IPC=50; CelebA: 68.50 vs. 74.09. Reviewers of related fairness-distillation work raised concerns about such accuracy degradation: *"the fairness is improved but in many cases at a price of degraded accuracy"* (MIuimtOu0T, Reviewer 4). The paper claims "without sacrificing classification accuracy" relative to vanilla DDs, but the broader accuracy-fairness trade-off relative to the original dataset deserves more transparent discussion.

